WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:02.000
You tap an app, and it answers instantly.

2
00:00:02.390 --> 00:00:03.690
It feels like nothing is there.

3
00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:07.240
But the cloud is a place, with walls, with power, and with an address.

4
00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:09.800
In June 2026,

5
00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:12.920
A fire broke out at a third-party data center in Delhi.

6
00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.020
Google's servers were not the problem.

7
00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:20.770
But a network junction there, called a point of presence, was forced offline by an emergency power shutdown.

8
00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.130
Google has not said what started the fire.

9
00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:26.880
That junction is where traffic enters Google's network.

10
00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:30.470
When it went dark, people in Delhi, Chennai, and Mumbai felt it.

11
00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:34.300
Connections slowed down, and some data may have been lost along the way.

12
00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:36.870
Google rerouted the traffic.

13
00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:39.780
But the detours were narrower than the road they replaced.

14
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.850
Demand was bigger than capacity, so traffic queued up.

15
00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:45.530
And for a while, Google said, there was no workaround.

16
00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:47.880
Recovery took weeks.

17
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:51.500
Google added capacity, route by route, in Delhi, and then in Chennai.

18
00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:54.560
Crews reached the damaged site on June 22.

19
00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:57.220
By June 26, service was back to normal.

20
00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:00.290
A backup only works if it has room.

21
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:01.810
The cloud is not weightless.

22
00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:03.870
It is buildings, power, and cables.

23
00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:05.540
And every one of them has an address.
